console: preserve the final newline when printk truncates a message

The Galaxy Tab S8 Ultra command line exceeds printk's 256-byte buffer.
Truncation drops its final newline, leaving simplefb's cursor on the last
wrapped line. The following "Booting kernel..." prefix then overwrites it.

When a truncated format ends in a newline, retain that newline in the last
available buffer byte. Keep the buffer size and existing short/unterminated
message behavior unchanged. This avoids increasing the loader's BSS or
changing its payload/stack addresses just to fix console formatting.

Generated against uniLoader 43770a0 with the existing port patch stack.
A host harness using the actual console formatter reproduces the missing
newline before this change and checks truncated, short and unterminated logs.
Both board builds retain the payload/BSS/relocation addresses. On SM-X900,
"Booting kernel..." stays on its own line and Linux text and USB still work.

diff --git a/lib/console/console.c b/lib/console/console.c
index 652ac7a..034ba1b 100644
--- a/lib/console/console.c
+++ b/lib/console/console.c
@@ -87,11 +87,20 @@ void printk(int log_level, const char *fmt, ...)
 
 	va_list args;
 	static char print_buffer[PRINTK_BUFFER_SIZE];
+	int length;
 
 	va_start(args, fmt);
-	npf_vsnprintf(print_buffer, sizeof(print_buffer), fmt, args);
+	length = npf_vsnprintf(print_buffer, sizeof(print_buffer), fmt, args);
 	va_end(args);
 
+	/* Keep the next console prefix off a truncated message's last line. */
+	if (length >= (int)sizeof(print_buffer)) {
+		size_t fmt_length = strlen(fmt);
+
+		if (fmt_length && fmt[fmt_length - 1] == '\n')
+			print_buffer[sizeof(print_buffer) - 2] = '\n';
+	}
+
 	const char *prefix = log_prefix(log_level);
 
 	for (unsigned int i = 0; i < n_consoles; i++)
